Go.dot patch 0005 for JUCE: a PNG or GIF with impossible numbers is refused before it is decoded.

JUCE's own fixes, in 8.0.17-security and 9.0.5, cherry-picked from JUCE's develop:
  ceb1b3f0717f "PNGLoader: Validate image dimensions before allocating the decode
               buffer" (ab9047fb26f2 on develop, Tom Poole, 2026-10-27)
  3d969a1e0fc3 "GIFLoader: Validate the LZW minimum code size and first code"
               (b90de9702124 on develop, Tom Poole, 2026-10-26)
Each develop commit applies cleanly to this pin (9.0.4+26, which they follow),
with Go.dot's notice after JUCE's licence block.

The faults: a PNG's width and height sized the decode buffer unchecked, so the
size could wrap and the decode write past it, and a failed allocation was not
noticed; a GIF's LZW minimum code size and first code were trusted, indexing
past the decoder's tables. Go.dot loads pictures through juce::ImageFileFormat
for image cues (VideoRenderChild.cpp), the media analyser and QLab imports.

What changes for Go.dot: nothing for a real picture; such a file loads as no
picture, which a cue already reports.

LINE ENDINGS. JUCE stores these files with CRLF in its own repository, so every
line of the hunks below ends in CRLF and must stay so; .gitattributes keeps this
file byte for byte (-text), and scripts/patches.py writes it as bytes.

Applied by Go.dot's build, cmake/WfgJucePatches.cmake, in the order
patches/JUCE/series gives; refreshed from the working tree with
scripts/patches.py JUCE refresh, which keeps this header. Against JUCE
7ff644f65d7d (9.0.4+26, the JUCE Tracktion Engine a5e44996f5f pins), where the
fixes are JUCE develop's own commits, cherry-picked. Drop it when the pin moves
to 9.0.5 or later. The author's decision of 2026-11-02.

